        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains the focal point of an intensifying maritime and diplomatic crisis. PortWatch recorded 2 transits on 2026-08-02, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that figure carries a multi-day lag given PortWatch's weekly Tuesday publication cycle and should not be read as current throughput. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports in the most recent 24 hours totaled 499 vessels, a figure collected under a different methodology and not comparable to the PortWatch transit count. Brent crude stood at $83.93, up +5.64% over 24 hours, as of 08:53 UTC. Ibtimes.co.in and globalsecurity.org both allege, without independent corroboration, that Iran struck a UAE ADNOC tanker with missiles; globalsecurity.org separately reports the League of Arab States condemned the alleged targeting and held Iran responsible. Neither claim has been confirmed by independent maritime sources. The Iranian Guards stated publicly that Hormuz will not reopen unless the United States meets Tehran's conditions, per thehindu.com and bangkokpost.com. Chennaionline.com and dunyanews.tv, both unverified single sources, report Iran and Oman are near a deal, though Iran's parallel public stance undercuts any straightforward read of near-term resolution. The site's Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast composite, a horizon-mix measure, has risen into the elevated band, driven primarily by Polymarket escalation contracts. Thirteen indexed events were recorded in the past 24 hours.",
